    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Environment and Recycling</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> You Can Save Water By...</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Analyze informational texts for specific purposes. Reading: Interpret characters' motives in short stories. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by pronouncing the past tense verbs ending "ed."</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can analyze informational texts for specific purposes, such as recycling tips (e.g., "Identify two ways to save water from the text.")
            Can interpret motives behind actions in short texts about sustainability (e.g., "Why should we recycle?").
            Can pronounce past tense verbs ending in "ed" (e.g., produces the appropriate sounds /d/, /ɪd/, and /t/ in words like "saved," "wasted," and "watched").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e8f3f8;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and linking to the lesson's theme.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Display 3-4 images related to water usage (e.g., a running tap, someone taking a short shower, a garden hose watering plants in the sun vs. a watering can in the early morning). Ask students: "What do you see in these pictures? Which actions help the `environment`? Which actions `waste` water?" Encourage using vocabulary like `save`, `protect`, `resource`, `pollution` (from previous lesson).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to a picture of someone turning off the tap and says, "We `should` always turn off the tap when brushing our teeth. In the `past`, many people didn't `save` water this way, but `today` we are more aware." The teacher models the pronunciation of `saved` (/d/) and `wasted` (/ɪd/) within these sentences.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> "Is leaving the water running good for our `planet`?" (No, it isn't.) "Why `should` we `save` water?" (To `protect` our `resources` for the future.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">_ _ / _ _ (5-7 min)</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to "The Environment and Recycling" through authentic text.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Material:</b> Provide students with a short informational text (e.g., a "How-to Guide" or "Tips for a Greener Home" article) titled "Simple Ways You Can `Save` Water." The text will be concise, include bullet points or short paragraphs, and integrate target grammar (Past simple vs. present practices, First conditional, Modal `should`) and vocabulary (`recycle`, `waste`, `environment`, `pollution`, `energy`, `planet`, `resource`, `reduce`, `reuse`, `clean`, `save`, `protect`, `environmental`, `important`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Analyze informational texts for specific purposes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher Action:</b> Introduce the text: "Today, we will `read` an informational text that gives us `important` tips on how we can `save` water and `protect` our `environment`."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Gist):</b> Ask students to read the title and first paragraph. "What is the main idea of this article?" (It's about ways to `save` water.) "Who is the article written `for`?" (Everyone, especially people who want to `protect` the `planet`.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Specific):</b> Students `read` the entire text. "Identify two specific ways mentioned in the text that help `save` water at home." (e.g., Turn off the tap, take shorter showers). "According to the text, why `should` we `reduce` water `waste`?" (Because `if` we `waste` water, there `will` be less `for` everyone, and it affects the `environment`.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">_ _ / _ _ (10-12 min)</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading skills, reinforcing language content.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading for specific information, interpreting meaning, and identifying cause-effect. Grammar: Past simple versus present practices; First conditional; Modal `should`. Vocabulary: `recycle`, `waste`, `environment`, `pollution`, `energy`, `landfill`, `planet`, `resource`, `reduce`, `reuse`, `clean`, `save`, `protect`, `environmental`, `clean`, `renewable`, `important`, `in`, `on`, `for`, `carefully`, `often`. Pronunciation: Past tense `-ed` endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                        <ol>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Gap-fill):</b> Provide a worksheet with sentences from the text, but with key `vocabulary` words or grammatical structures (e.g., `should`, `if`, past tense verbs) replaced with blanks. Students read the text again to fill in the missing words. E.g., "We _________ turn off the tap while brushing teeth." (`should`). "If we _________ water, there will be less `for` everyone." (`waste`). "In the _________, people didn't `recycle` as much." (`past`).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence matching/reordering):</b> Give students jumbled sentences or two halves of sentences related to advice or cause-effect from the text. Students must `read` and correctly match or order them. E.g., Match: "You `should` fix leaky faucets." with "to `save` hundreds of liters of water." Or "If you collect rainwater," with "you can use it `for` your plants."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check (Reading & Pronunciation):</b> In pairs, students take turns reading out loud sentences from the text that contain past simple verbs (e.g., "My grandmother `saved` all the rainwater."). The partner listens and identifies the past tense verb, then practices its pronunciation focusing on the `-ed` ending sound (/d/ in `saved`, /ɪd/ in `wasted`, /t/ in `watched`). They then discuss the meaning: "Why did her grandmother `save` rainwater? What does this tell us about her motives?"</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">_ _ / _ _ (15-18 min)</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario, focusing on fluency.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative group task: "Creating a Water-Saving Poster for Our Community."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Divide students into small groups (3-4).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Instruct each group to design a mini-poster or a set of illustrated cards that summarize the `important` water-saving tips they `read` about, and add 1-2 new ideas of their own `for` their local community.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students `should` write short sentences using the target grammar (e.g., "You `should` take shorter showers." "If we `save` water, our `planet` `will` be healthier." "In the `past`, many `wasted` water, but `today` we `protect` it.") and `vocabulary` from the lesson.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group presents their poster/cards to the class, `reading` their tips and explaining them. Emphasis is `on` clear communication and applying what they `read`.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">_ _ / _ _ (8-10 min)</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy to check learning and provide feedback.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Teacher observes student performance during the group poster presentation and discussions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies key information from the `reading` text (analyzes informational texts for specific purposes).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses modal `should` and `If... will` correctly when giving advice/cause-effect.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Incorporates at least 4 new `vocabulary` words appropriately (e.g., `save`, `waste`, `environment`, `protect`).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronounces past tense verbs ending `-ed` intelligibly, differentiating /d/, /ɪd/, /t/ (e.g., `saved`, `wasted`, `watched`).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates `effectively` within the group.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers on a small slip of paper:
                        <ol>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you `read` today that you `think` is `important` `for` `saving` water?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to understand the text and why?"</li>
                        </ol>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">_ _ / _ _ (5 min)</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and planning for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the information you `read` and the tips you discussed today help your family or community `protect` our `planet`'s `resources`?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of `reading` about `saving` water did you find most interesting or useful?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students achieve the `reading` objective of analyzing informational texts and interpreting motives? How effectively did they apply the strategies `learned`?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Were the activities `carefully` scaffolded `for` the A2.3 level? What modifications might be needed `for` students who struggled with text comprehension or pronunciation of `-ed` endings?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did the integration of grammar, vocabulary, and pronunciation work within the `reading` focus?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Great work today `reading` about `saving` water! In our next lesson (Lesson 3), we `will` `practice` speaking about `environmental` issues and `recycling` with our classmates, using the `important` vocabulary and grammar we `learned` in the `past` two lessons."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">_ _ / _ _ (5 min)</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>